What is entangled, and how far
------------------------------

A is one sub-series because of two patches that sit on three otherwise separate lines of work:

  * 0014 probe-version-bounds threads a version bound through _iter_match_pkgs_atom_imp()
    (created by 0002) and _iter_similar_available_imp() (created by 0005), stores its key in
    the memo object of 0013, and reads config._regenerate_warned, which 0012 sets for it.
  * 0029 probe-certain-atoms calls _probe_bounds_allowed() of 0014.
  * 0016 pkg-use-without-setcpv is built on the memo functions of 0012.
  * 0028 atom-key-caches changes the key of the memo of 0002 (two lines) and places its helper
    after the class that 0005 adds.

Inside A three branches stand alone and were measured alone (arms A-probe, A-match, A-config
above): 0001 0004 0005 0013 (probe memo), 0002 (match replay), 0012 0016 (config memos). What
each patch needs is written in its header ("Of these it needs ... only") and is listed here:

  A  0001 probe-memo                             needs nothing
  A  0002 match-replay                           needs nothing
  A  0004 probe-upgrade-downgrade-memo           needs 0001
  A  0005 probe-similar-lazy-replay              needs 0001
  A  0012 regenerate-memos                       needs nothing
  A  0013 probe-memo-object                      needs 0001 0004 0005
  A  0014 probe-version-bounds                   needs 0001 0002 0004 0005 0012 0013
  A  0016 pkg-use-without-setcpv                 needs 0012
  A  0028 atom-key-caches                        needs 0001 0002 0005
  A  0029 probe-certain-atoms                    needs 0001 0002 0004 0005 0012 0013 0014
  B  0003 mergeable-from-frontier                needs nothing
  B  0006 empty-cycle-search-memo                needs nothing
  B  0007 one-node-cycle-shortcut                needs nothing
  B  0010 frontier-leaf-sets                     needs 0003 0006
  B  0011 gather-ok-tracker                      needs 0003 0006 0007 0010
  B  0024 frontier-mergeable-set-node-removal    needs 0003 0006 0007 0010 0011
  C  0008 use-reduce-store                       needs nothing
  C  0018 c-parser-atom-memo                     needs 0008
  C  0030 use-reduce-store-rewrap                needs 0008
  D  0009 gc-threshold                           needs nothing
  D  0019 pretend-gc-off-fast-exit               needs 0009

A patch applies, and gives the tree the weave predicts, on pristine 3.0.83 plus exactly the
patches named for it (check.sh deps). Of these smaller sets only the three branches of A were
run through the gate here; 0001, 0002, 0003, 0006, 0007, 0008, 0009 and the pairs 0001+0004,
0001+0004+0005 were measured alone earlier (emerge-fast-upstream/pick/results.tsv, three
states), not again in this pass.

B is the cycle search of _serialize_tasks: 0010 rewrites the memo of 0006 and one line of 0003,
0011 rewrites part of 0007, 0024 replaces the reduced forms of 0003 and 0007.
C: 0018 calls the C parser from inside the function that 0008 adds; 0030 re-wraps a line of 0008.
D: 0019 turns the literal threshold of 0009 into a constant and adds the --pretend path.


What changed against the 25-patch ladder
----------------------------------------

Unchanged numbers and names, same change as before: 0001-0020, 0023, 0024.

Split:
  old 0021 atom-matching-caches  -> 0021 atom-matching-caches (portage/dep/__init__.py and
                                    portage/versions.py: one-atom match path, Atom slot
                                    descriptors, vercmp/catpkgsplit caches)        sub-series H
                                 -> 0028 atom-key-caches (all hunks in _emerge/: depgraph.py,
                                    PackageVirtualDbapi.py, resolver/package_tracker.py)   A
  old 0025 config-trims-and-leftovers
                                 -> 0022 required-use-memo (depgraph.py: the
                                    _required_use_sat_memo line and the _add_pkg hunk)      I
                                 -> 0025 config-trims (config.py, LicenseManager.py,
                                    UseManager.py)                                          K
                                 -> 0026 flatten-atoms-memo (depgraph.py: the
                                    _flatten_atoms_cache lines)                             L
                                 -> 0027 leftovers (Package.py, dep_check.py, dep_expand.py,
                                    digraph.py, the _pkg_use_enabled hunk of depgraph.py)   M
                                 -> 0030 use-reduce-store-rewrap (the one hunk in
                                    portage/dep/__init__.py)                                C
Moved:
  old 0022 probe-certain-atoms   -> 0029 (same change)                                      A

Nothing was merged, and no hunk moved between two patches that both still exist. Why the order
of the tail changed: required-use-memo, flatten-atoms-memo and probe-certain-atoms each add
lines at one and the same place of _frozen_depgraph_config.__init__, and in the tested tree
they stand in this order. Each can apply without the others only if it is the lowest block at
that place when it is applied, hence 0022 < 0026 < 0029. Trees after 19 to 29 patches therefore
differ from the old trees after 19 to 24; none of them is a ladder prefix.

Ladder prefixes (ebuilds 9999-r1 .. -r8): 1, 3, 9, 11, 14, 16, 18 patches as before; the last
step is now "all 30" instead of "all 25". No prefix tree changed, so nothing was re-measured.


How the hunks are cut
---------------------

Written by tools/recut.py from a weave of the original series (tools/weave.py: every line with
the patch that adds it and the patch that deletes it). A patch must apply to several trees (the
full chain, its sub-series alone, its own dependencies alone, its sub-series with any other
one), and patch(1) wants the same number of context lines before and after a hunk, so each hunk
has as many context lines (3 at most) as are identical on both sides in all those trees.

Reduced context (2 lines instead of 3), 4 hunks, each checked to land where it should:
  0010 _serialize_frontier.py  the ready_nodes() hunk
  0015 depgraph.py             the _find_single_atom() helper; the findAtomForPackage hunk in
                               _iter_match_pkgs_atom
  0026 depgraph.py             the second _flatten_atoms_cache assignment

"Anchor" hunks, 6: a pure insertion next to lines of another sub-series has no context line that
is the same in every tree on both sides. It is written without context as a replacement of the
one neighbouring line that IS the same in every tree, re-stating that line:

    -        self._highest_license_masked = {}
    +        # (pkg, enabled USE flags) -> bool, see depgraph._add_pkg.
    +        self._required_use_sat_memo = {}
    +        self._highest_license_masked = {}

The re-stated line occurs once in the file in every tree that was tried, so the hunk cannot land
elsewhere. These are in 0006 (first hunk in the selection loop of _serialize_tasks; needed so
that 0006 applies without 0003), 0022, 0023, 0026, 0028, 0029 (helpers and memo attributes that
several sub-series add at the same two places of depgraph.py). Two hunks at the end of a file
(0017 Dependency.py, 0023 AbstractDepPriority.py) have no trailing context because there is none.


Checks (tools/check.sh; rebuilds nothing)
-----------------------------------------

    tools/check.sh all        # full, alone, deps, pairs      -> check.log
    tools/check.sh leaveout

  full      30 patches in order on pristine source, patch -p1 -F0: equal to the tested tree
            (emerge-fast-ebuild/b/lib) and to the 25-patch ladder; prefixes 1 3 9 11 14 16 18
            equal to the same prefixes of the 25-patch ladder; byte-compile after every patch
  alone     each sub-series alone on pristine source: applies with -F0, equals the tree the
            weave predicts for that set of patches, changed files byte-compile
  deps      each patch on pristine source plus only the patches it is said to need: same tests
  pairs     every two sub-series together in numeric order: same tests; 78 pairs
  leaveout  the series without one sub-series, 13 times: same tests

Result of the last run: see check.log (all PASS, pairs "78 tried, 0 not clean").
Not checked by these scripts: three or more sub-series together other than the full series and
the 13 leave-one-out sets.


Measurements (tools/run-arms.sh, tools/summary.py; results.tsv has every run)
----------------------------------------------------------------------------

    tools/run-arms.sh build          # arms/NAME = installed stock 3.0.83 files + the patches
    tools/run-arms.sh run p1         # six states x (stock + every arm of tools/arms.txt)
    tools/summary.py p1

Test bed: ~/Claude/qagent/emerge-fast (scenario.sh, apply-run.sh), image rootfs read-only, one
resolve at a time, 1-minute load recorded at the start and end of every run (columns 9 and 10
of results.tsv); no run was started with a load above 3.5. Raw output of every run: out/p1/.
The stock merge lists of this pass equal the stored reference lists of the ladder run
(emerge-fast-upstream/ladder/out/STATE.stock.order) for all six states.

Pass p1, 2026-11-21 08:26-10:37: 102 runs (6 states x stock + 16 arms), all with gate ok and
status ok. Highest 1-minute load at the start of a run 3.38; one run (start, arm L) ended with a
load of 4.43 after starting at 2.65 and was not repeated (its figure, 122.3 s, is the stock
figure). The desktop was in use during the pass (load about 2 throughout), so take the seconds
as indicative. A "smoke" pass of two runs (holds: stock, A) precedes p1 in results.tsv.
Not done: second runs, the full series as an arm (its tree is the tested tree, measured in the
ladder run), the gate for dependency sets other than the three branches of A.


Files
-----

  patches/          the 30 patch files
  README            this file (tools/mkreadme.py writes it from tools/README.in and results.tsv)
  results.tsv       pass state arm resolve_s wall_s backtracks packages gate load_start load_end
                    status started patches
  check.log         output of tools/check.sh all leaveout
  build.log         output of tools/recut.py build (the hunks with reduced context)
  out/PASS/         STATE.ARM.{json,log,order,err} of every run
  arms/             the candidate trees that were measured
  tools/plan.py     the cut: units, order, sub-series, dependencies
  tools/weave.py    the weave of the original series; `weave.py deps` lists who rewrites whom
  tools/recut.py    writes patches/ (build), a predicted tree (view), the tables (info)
  tools/check.sh    the checks
  tools/run-arms.sh, tools/arms.txt, tools/summary.py     the measurement
  tools/headers/    header texts of the patches that were split off
  tools/show.py, tools/idents.py   helpers used while cutting (weave regions of a patch; names
                    one patch introduces and another uses)

Origin: the patches were written by AI agents (see each header); this re-cut was made by an AI
agent as well (Claude, 2026-11-21) and changes no line of code.
